1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,840
Good morning! Today I want to compare
three major renewable energy sources,

2
00:00:05,164 --> 00:00:09,220
solar, wind and geothermal, using
the three tables on your handout.

3
00:00:09,674 --> 00:00:11,640
Let's start with the key figures table.

4
00:00:11,974 --> 00:00:15,640
Solar panels have a typical
lifespan of around 25 years.

5
00:00:16,055 --> 00:00:19,680
Their main advantage is low maintenance,
since there are no moving parts.

6
00:00:20,095 --> 00:00:23,040
Their main limitation is that
output depends on weather.

7
00:00:23,365 --> 00:00:25,680
Heavy cloud can cut
production dramatically.

8
00:00:26,180 --> 00:00:28,754
Wind turbines typically
last about 20 years, and

9
00:00:28,778 --> 00:00:31,312
their running costs are
low once they're built.

10
00:00:31,760 --> 00:00:34,104
The limitation people
complain about most is

11
00:00:34,128 --> 00:00:36,592
turbine noise, especially
those living nearby.

12
00:00:37,060 --> 00:00:39,888
Geothermal plants last
longer, around 30 years,

13
00:00:39,912 --> 00:00:42,752
and they give a constant
output, day and night.

14
00:00:43,165 --> 00:00:45,945
But geothermal is only
possible in limited locations,

15
00:00:45,969 --> 00:00:48,972
where heat from the earth is
close enough to the surface.

16
00:00:49,360 --> 00:00:53,420
Now let's move to the second table,
global installed capacity by region.

17
00:00:53,869 --> 00:00:57,006
In Asia, solar is the
leading source, and solar

18
00:00:57,030 --> 00:00:59,912
capacity there grew
by 20% last year alone.

19
00:01:00,300 --> 00:01:04,420
In Europe, wind is the leading source,
with especially strong offshore growth.

20
00:01:04,819 --> 00:01:07,746
In North America, wind
also leads, and that growth

21
00:01:07,770 --> 00:01:10,532
has been led by one
state in particular, Texas.

22
00:01:11,159 --> 00:01:15,780
In Africa, solar leads, but expansion
has been held back by infrastructure costs.

23
00:01:16,049 --> 00:01:18,520
Above all, the cost of
building transmission lines.

24
00:01:19,220 --> 00:01:22,958
And in South America, at least in the
Andean countries covered by our data,

25
00:01:22,982 --> 00:01:27,232
geothermal is the leading source, because
the region benefits from volcanic activity.

26
00:01:27,654 --> 00:01:31,260
Let's move now to our third table,
the environmental impact notes.

27
00:01:31,760 --> 00:01:34,394
On land use, large solar
farms need large areas

28
00:01:34,418 --> 00:01:36,952
of flat land, which can
compete with farming.

29
00:01:37,399 --> 00:01:39,669
On wildlife, wind
turbines can affect bird

30
00:01:39,693 --> 00:01:42,572
populations, since birds
can collide with the blades.

31
00:01:42,999 --> 00:01:47,740
On water use, geothermal plants use water
for cooling, which matters in dry regions.

32
00:01:48,179 --> 00:01:52,460
On emissions, all three technologies
produce zero emissions during operation.

33
00:01:52,920 --> 00:01:56,426
On manufacturing, solar panels
require rare earth mining for some of

34
00:01:56,450 --> 00:02:00,072
their components, and that mining
has its own environmental footprint.

35
00:02:00,579 --> 00:02:03,072
On recycling, wind turbine
blades are difficult to

36
00:02:03,096 --> 00:02:05,852
recycle, because they're
made from composite materials.

37
00:02:06,389 --> 00:02:09,218
On decommissioning,
geothermal sites need long-term

38
00:02:09,242 --> 00:02:12,472
monitoring after they close,
to check for ground movement.

39
00:02:12,980 --> 00:02:16,668
On public perception, wind
farms face objections over their

40
00:02:16,692 --> 00:02:20,392
appearance, particularly in
scenic coastal and rural areas.

41
00:02:20,800 --> 00:02:23,670
On government support,
most countries offer financial

42
00:02:23,694 --> 00:02:27,012
subsidies to help with the
high upfront cost of installation.

43
00:02:27,484 --> 00:02:30,100
And finally, the future
outlook is positive.

44
00:02:30,564 --> 00:02:35,220
Costs are expected to keep falling, making
renewables more competitive every year.

45
00:02:35,570 --> 00:02:38,093
Next week, we'll look at
how these three sources

46
00:02:38,117 --> 00:02:40,812
can be combined in a
balanced national energy grid.

